        {
            "id": "33041397",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "03:41, 4 January 2025",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,897 bytes)",
            "change": 252,
            "summary": "(Added expert citations.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Stop the fire before it starts. The majority of electrical fires stem from faulty electrical wiring or poor maintenance of electrical systems.<ref>[v162501_b01]. 18 January 2024.</ref> To stop an electrical fire before it starts, do not overload electrical outlets and ensure that all electrical work is performed to code by a licensed electrician.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off power to the electrical system. If an electrical system begins sparking or a fire ignites at a wire, appliance, or outlet, then cutting the power to the system is the first, best step to take.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> If the source is only sparking or the flame has not yet spread at all, this step alone may be enough to extinguish the flame.<ref>[v162501_b01]. 18 January 2024.</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. Alternatively, you can use a fire blanket to smother the fire, but this step is only applicable if you were able to turn off the power to the source completely.<ref>[v162501_b01]. 18 January 2024.</ref> Though wool (most fire blankets are chemically treated wool) is a good insulator of electricity, you still don’t want to get close enough to the source and risk electrocution if the power remains on.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use water to extinguish the fire. If you don’t have any type of fire extinguisher or fire blanket around, then you can use water; however, ONLY use water if you have 100% turned the power to the source OFF. Otherwise you not only risk electrocution, but also spreading around the electricity, which can spread the fire much more quickly. Throw the water at the base or seat of the fire.<ref>[v162501_b01]. 18 January 2024.</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. You can also use a fire blanket on small Class B fires. If a fire blanket is readily available, this can be the easiest, least damaging method to extinguish it.<ref>[v162501_b01]. 18 January 2024.</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to extinguish the fire. If the fuel source for the fire is a solid combustible material—wood, cloth, paper, rubber, plastic, etc.—then you have a Class A fire. A fire blanket is a quick, easy way to extinguish the initial stage of a Class A fire. The fire blanket removes oxygen from the fire, which starves the fire of it’s ability to burn.<ref>[v162501_b01]. 18 January 2024.</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Stop the fire before it starts. The majority of electrical fires stem from faulty electrical wiring or poor maintenance of electrical systems. To stop an electrical fire before it starts, do not overload electrical outlets and ensure that all electrical work is performed to code by a licensed electrician.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off power to the electrical system. If an electrical system begins sparking or a fire ignites at a wire, appliance, or outlet, then cutting the power to the system is the first, best step to take.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> If the source is only sparking or the flame has not yet spread at all, this step alone may be enough to extinguish the flame.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. Alternatively, you can use a fire blanket to smother the fire, but this step is only applicable if you were able to turn off the power to the source completely. Though wool (most fire blankets are chemically treated wool) is a good insulator of electricity, you still don’t want to get close enough to the source and risk electrocution if the power remains on.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use water to extinguish the fire. If you don’t have any type of fire extinguisher or fire blanket around, then you can use water; however, ONLY use water if you have 100% turned the power to the source OFF. Otherwise you not only risk electrocution, but also spreading around the electricity, which can spread the fire much more quickly. Throw the water at the base or seat of the fire.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. You can also use a fire blanket on small Class B fires. If a fire blanket is readily available, this can be the easiest, least damaging method to extinguish it.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to extinguish the fire. If the fuel source for the fire is a solid combustible material—wood, cloth, paper, rubber, plastic, etc.—then you have a Class A fire. A fire blanket is a quick, easy way to extinguish the initial stage of a Class A fire. The fire blanket removes oxygen from the fire, which starves the fire of it’s ability to burn.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "32977662",
            "user": "WikiHow Horizon 3",
            "timestamp": "19:23, 4 December 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,645 bytes)",
            "change": 9,
            "summary": "(http ► https)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Stop the fire before it starts. The majority of electrical fires stem from faulty electrical wiring or poor maintenance of electrical systems. To stop an electrical fire before it starts, do not overload electrical outlets and ensure that all electrical work is performed to code by a licensed electrician.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Also keep electrical systems clear of dust, trash, and spider webs, which can all lead to fires.<ref>https://nasdonline.org/document/978/d000818/electrical-fires-prevention-and-extinguishing.html</ref>",
                    "#* You should also use circuit breakers and fuses as often as possible, which are easy steps to take in order to stop a power surge from starting a fire.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>",
                    "#Turn off power to the electrical system. If an electrical system begins sparking or a fire ignites at a wire, appliance, or outlet, then cutting the power to the system is the first, best step to take.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> If the source is only sparking or the flame has not yet spread at all, this step alone may be enough to extinguish the flame.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You should cut the power at the breaker box rather than turning off the wall switch connected to the outlet.<ref>https://www.networx.com/article/electrical-sparks-from-outlet</ref>",
                    "#Use a Class C-rated extinguisher if you cannot cut the power to the source. The acceptable type of fire extinguisher in this situation depends entirely upon if you are able to cut the power to the source or not. If you don’t know where the breaker is, the box is locked, or it would simply take way too long to access, then you must use a Class C-rated fire extinguisher.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> Class C extinguishers are either carbon dioxide (CO2) or dry chemical extinguishers, and they will specifically include “Class C” on the label on the canister.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You must use a Class C extinguisher because they contain non-conductive substances. A Class A extinguisher will only contain highly pressurized water, which conducts electricity and can create electrocution risks.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>",
                    "#Use a Class A or dry chemical extinguisher if you have cut the power. If you are able to cut the power to the source completely, then you have turned the Class C electrical fire into a standard Class A fire.<ref>https://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> In this case, you can use a water-based Class A extinguisher in addition to the previously mentioned extinguishers.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Stop the fire before it starts. The majority of electrical fires stem from faulty electrical wiring or poor maintenance of electrical systems. To stop an electrical fire before it starts, do not overload electrical outlets and ensure that all electrical work is performed to code by a licensed electrician.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Also keep electrical systems clear of dust, trash, and spider webs, which can all lead to fires.<ref>http://nasdonline.org/document/978/d000818/electrical-fires-prevention-and-extinguishing.html</ref>",
                    "#* You should also use circuit breakers and fuses as often as possible, which are easy steps to take in order to stop a power surge from starting a fire.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>",
                    "#Turn off power to the electrical system. If an electrical system begins sparking or a fire ignites at a wire, appliance, or outlet, then cutting the power to the system is the first, best step to take.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> If the source is only sparking or the flame has not yet spread at all, this step alone may be enough to extinguish the flame.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You should cut the power at the breaker box rather than turning off the wall switch connected to the outlet.<ref>http://www.networx.com/article/electrical-sparks-from-outlet</ref>",
                    "#Use a Class C-rated extinguisher if you cannot cut the power to the source. The acceptable type of fire extinguisher in this situation depends entirely upon if you are able to cut the power to the source or not. If you don’t know where the breaker is, the box is locked, or it would simply take way too long to access, then you must use a Class C-rated fire extinguisher.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> Class C extinguishers are either carbon dioxide (CO2) or dry chemical extinguishers, and they will specifically include “Class C” on the label on the canister.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* You must use a Class C extinguisher because they contain non-conductive substances. A Class A extinguisher will only contain highly pressurized water, which conducts electricity and can create electrocution risks.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>",
                    "#Use a Class A or dry chemical extinguisher if you have cut the power. If you are able to cut the power to the source completely, then you have turned the Class C electrical fire into a standard Class A fire.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> In this case, you can use a water-based Class A extinguisher in addition to the previously mentioned extinguishers.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
