            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#* Another way to identify CO2 and dry chemical extinguishers is by their red coloring (water extinguishers are silver). CO2 extinguishers also have a hard horn at the tip instead of just a hose, and they lack a pressure gauge.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/evacuation-plans-procedures</ref>",
                    "#* Class A extinguishers and multipurpose dry chemical extinguishers are actually recommended in this scenario because CO2 extinguishers run a higher risk of the fire smoldering and reigniting once the CO2 dissipates.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/evacuation-plans-procedures</ref> CO2 extinguishers can also cause respiratory problems in confined spaces such as houses or small offices.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/evacuation-plans-procedures</ref>",
                    "#Use a Class B fire extinguisher. As with electrical fires, water-based (Class A) fire extinguishers should not be used on liquid or oil fires. Carbon dioxide (CO2) and dry chemical fire extinguishers will have a Class B rating.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/evacuation-plans-procedures</ref> Check the label on the extinguisher and ensure that it says Class B before using it on a flammable liquid fire.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Solely Class A extinguishers will be silver and will have a pressure gauge for the water inside; however, many multipurpose dry chemical extinguishers will also be rated for Class A fires.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/etools/evacuation-plans-procedures</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#* Another way to identify CO2 and dry chemical extinguishers is by their red coloring (water extinguishers are silver). CO2 extinguishers also have a hard horn at the tip instead of just a hose, and they lack a pressure gauge.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/evacuation/portable_about.html#co2</ref>",
                    "#* Class A extinguishers and multipurpose dry chemical extinguishers are actually recommended in this scenario because CO2 extinguishers run a higher risk of the fire smoldering and reigniting once the CO2 dissipates.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/evacuation/portable_about.html#co2</ref> CO2 extinguishers can also cause respiratory problems in confined spaces such as houses or small offices.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/evacuation/portable_about.html#co2</ref>",
                    "#Use a Class B fire extinguisher. As with electrical fires, water-based (Class A) fire extinguishers should not be used on liquid or oil fires. Carbon dioxide (CO2) and dry chemical fire extinguishers will have a Class B rating.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/evacuation/portable_about.html</ref> Check the label on the extinguisher and ensure that it says Class B before using it on a flammable liquid fire.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Solely Class A extinguishers will be silver and will have a pressure gauge for the water inside; however, many multipurpose dry chemical extinguishers will also be rated for Class A fires.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/evacuation/portable_about.html</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
