        {
            "id": "20647412",
            "user": "WikiHow Projects",
            "timestamp": "09:24, 24 July 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(16,497 bytes)",
            "change": -840,
            "summary": "(removing broken link)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. Alternatively, you can use a fire blanket to smother the fire, but this step is only applicable if you were able to turn off the power to the source completely. Though wool (most fire blankets are chemically treated wool) is a good insulator of electricity, you still don’t want to get close enough to the source and risk electrocution if the power remains on.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* To use a fire blanket, remove from its packing, grip the unfolded blanket in front of you with your hands and body protected by it, and drape the blanket over the small fire. Do NOT throw the blanket at the fire.",
                    "#* To use a fire blanket, remove from its packing, grip the unfolded blanket in front of you with your hands and body protected by it, and drape the blanket over the small fire. Do NOT throw the blanket at the fire.",
                    "#* To use a fire blanket, remove from its packing, grip the unfolded blanket in front of you with your hands and body protected by it, and drape the blanket over the small fire. Do NOT throw the blanket at the fire."
                ],
                "removed": [
