        {
            "id": "17392335",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "13:08, 21 May 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(17,262 bytes)",
            "change": 322,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Stop the fire before it starts. The majority of electrical fires stem from faulty electrical wiring or poor maintenance of electrical systems. To stop an electrical fire before it starts, do not overload electrical outlets and ensure that all electrical work is performed to code by a licensed electrician.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off power to the electrical system. If an electrical system begins sparking or a fire ignites at a wire, appliance, or outlet, then cutting the power to the system is the first, best step to take.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> If the source is only sparking or the flame has not yet spread at all, this step alone may be enough to extinguish the flame.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a Class C-rated extinguisher if you cannot cut the power to the source. The acceptable type of fire extinguisher in this situation depends entirely upon if you are able to cut the power to the source or not. If you don’t know where the breaker is, the box is locked, or it would simply take way too long to access, then you must use a Class C-rated fire extinguisher.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> Class C extinguishers are either carbon dioxide (CO2) or dry chemical extinguishers, and they will specifically include “Class C” on the label on the canister.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a Class A or dry chemical extinguisher if you have cut the power. If you are able to cut the power to the source completely, then you have turned the Class C electrical fire into a standard Class A fire.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> In this case, you can use a water-based Class A extinguisher in addition to the previously mentioned extinguishers.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. Alternatively, you can use a fire blanket to smother the fire, but this step is only applicable if you were able to turn off the power to the source completely.<ref>http://www.mfs.sa.gov.au/site/community_safety/home_fire_safety_information_sheets_domestic/fire_blankets.jsp</ref> Though wool (most fire blankets are chemically treated wool) is a good insulator of electricity, you still don’t want to get close enough to the source and risk electrocution if the power remains on.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use water to extinguish the fire. If you don’t have any type of fire extinguisher or fire blanket around, then you can use water; however, ONLY use water if you have 100% turned the power to the source OFF. Otherwise you not only risk electrocution, but also spreading around the electricity, which can spread the fire much more quickly. Throw the water at the base or seat of the fire.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call 911. Even if the fire is out, you should still call 911.<ref>http://emilms.fema.gov/IS909/assets/09_PuttingOutFires.pdf</ref> Smoldering objects can reignite, and proper firefighters will be able to isolate and remove any risks completely.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Shut off the fuel supply. In applicable situations, the first thing you should do for fires involving flammable liquids is to turn the fuel supply off.<ref>http://www.utexas.edu/safety/fire/extinguishers/abcs.html</ref> For instance, if a static discharge ignites gasoline around a fuel pump, the first thing you should do is hit the emergency shutoff valve located nearby at all pumping stations. This act severs the small fire from the very large fuel sources around it.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. You can also use a fire blanket on small Class B fires. If a fire blanket is readily available, this can be the easiest, least damaging method to extinguish it.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a Class B fire extinguisher. As with electrical fires, water-based (Class A) fire extinguishers should not be used on liquid or oil fires. Carbon dioxide (CO2) and dry chemical fire extinguishers will have a Class B rating.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/evacuation/portable_about.html</ref> Check the label on the extinguisher and ensure that it says Class B before using it on a flammable liquid fire.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call 911. Even if the fire is out, you should still call 911.<ref>http://emilms.fema.gov/IS909/assets/09_PuttingOutFires.pdf</ref> Smoldering objects can reignite, and proper firefighters will be able to isolate and remove any risks completely.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a fire blanket to extinguish the fire. If the fuel source for the fire is a solid combustible material—wood, cloth, paper, rubber, plastic, etc.—then you have a Class A fire.<ref>http://www.utexas.edu/safety/fire/extinguishers/abcs.html</ref> A fire blanket is a quick, easy way to extinguish the initial stage of a Class A fire. The fire blanket removes oxygen from the fire, which starves the fire of it’s ability to burn.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use a Class A fire extinguisher on the fire. If you do not have a fire blanket handy, then you can easily use a fire extinguisher on a Class A fire. Ensure that the label on the extinguisher reads Class A.<ref>http://www.utexas.edu/safety/fire/extinguishers/abcs.html</ref>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Use plenty of water. A specifically Class A fire extinguisher is essentially water under pressure, so you can use large amounts of water from a sink if it’s the only thing you have available. If the fire is obviously spreading faster than you can douse it—or if it’s producing too much smoke for you to try safely—then you must evacuate the space and call 911 instead.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call 911. As with any type of fire, you can to call 911 even if you manage to extinguish the fire. Emergency responders will ensure that the fire has no chance of reigniting.[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 15.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Stop the fire before it starts. The majority of electrical fires stem from faulty electrical wiring or poor maintenance of electrical systems. To stop an electrical fire before it starts, do not overload electrical outlets and ensure that all electrical work is performed to code by a licensed electrician.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref>",
                    "# Turn off power to the electrical system. If an electrical system begins sparking or a fire ignites at a wire, appliance, or outlet, then cutting the power to the system is the first, best step to take.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> If the source is only sparking or the flame has not yet spread at all, this step alone may be enough to extinguish the flame.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Use a Class C-rated extinguisher if you cannot cut the power to the source. The acceptable type of fire extinguisher in this situation depends entirely upon if you are able to cut the power to the source or not. If you don’t know where the breaker is, the box is locked, or it would simply take way too long to access, then you must use a Class C-rated fire extinguisher.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> Class C extinguishers are either carbon dioxide (CO2) or dry chemical extinguishers, and they will specifically include “Class C” on the label on the canister.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref><br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Use a Class A or dry chemical extinguisher if you have cut the power. If you are able to cut the power to the source completely, then you have turned the Class C electrical fire into a standard Class A fire.<ref>http://www.fire-extinguisher101.com/class-c-fires.html</ref> In this case, you can use a water-based Class A extinguisher in addition to the previously mentioned extinguishers.",
                    "# Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. Alternatively, you can use a fire blanket to smother the fire, but this step is only applicable if you were able to turn off the power to the source completely.<ref>http://www.mfs.sa.gov.au/site/community_safety/home_fire_safety_information_sheets_domestic/fire_blankets.jsp</ref> Though wool (most fire blankets are chemically treated wool) is a good insulator of electricity, you still don’t want to get close enough to the source and risk electrocution if the power remains on.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Use water to extinguish the fire. If you don’t have any type of fire extinguisher or fire blanket around, then you can use water; however, ONLY use water if you have 100% turned the power to the source OFF. Otherwise you not only risk electrocution, but also spreading around the electricity, which can spread the fire much more quickly. Throw the water at the base or seat of the fire.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Call 911. Even if the fire is out, you should still call 911.<ref>http://emilms.fema.gov/IS909/assets/09_PuttingOutFires.pdf</ref> Smoldering objects can reignite, and proper firefighters will be able to isolate and remove any risks completely.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Shut off the fuel supply. In applicable situations, the first thing you should do for fires involving flammable liquids is to turn the fuel supply off.<ref>http://www.utexas.edu/safety/fire/extinguishers/abcs.html</ref> For instance, if a static discharge ignites gasoline around a fuel pump, the first thing you should do is hit the emergency shutoff valve located nearby at all pumping stations. This act severs the small fire from the very large fuel sources around it.",
                    "# Use a fire blanket to smother the fire. You can also use a fire blanket on small Class B fires. If a fire blanket is readily available, this can be the easiest, least damaging method to extinguish it.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Use a Class B fire extinguisher. As with electrical fires, water-based (Class A) fire extinguishers should not be used on liquid or oil fires. Carbon dioxide (CO2) and dry chemical fire extinguishers will have a Class B rating.<ref>https://www.osha.gov/SLTC/etools/evacuation/portable_about.html</ref> Check the label on the extinguisher and ensure that it says Class B before using it on a flammable liquid fire.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Call 911. Even if the fire is out, you should still call 911.<ref>http://emilms.fema.gov/IS909/assets/09_PuttingOutFires.pdf</ref> Smoldering objects can reignite, and proper firefighters will be able to isolate and remove any risks completely.",
                    "# Use a fire blanket to extinguish the fire. If the fuel source for the fire is a solid combustible material—wood, cloth, paper, rubber, plastic, etc.—then you have a Class A fire.<ref>http://www.utexas.edu/safety/fire/extinguishers/abcs.html</ref> A fire blanket is a quick, easy way to extinguish the initial stage of a Class A fire. The fire blanket removes oxygen from the fire, which starves the fire of it’s ability to burn.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Use a Class A fire extinguisher on the fire. If you do not have a fire blanket handy, then you can easily use a fire extinguisher on a Class A fire. Ensure that the label on the extinguisher reads Class A.<ref>http://www.utexas.edu/safety/fire/extinguishers/abcs.html</ref><br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Use plenty of water. A specifically Class A fire extinguisher is essentially water under pressure, so you can use large amounts of water from a sink if it’s the only thing you have available. If the fire is obviously spreading faster than you can douse it—or if it’s producing too much smoke for you to try safely—then you must evacuate the space and call 911 instead.<br><br>[[Image:Extinguish a Fire at the Initial Stages Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Call 911. As with any type of fire, you can to call 911 even if you manage to extinguish the fire. Emergency responders will ensure that the fire has no chance of reigniting."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "17333658",
            "user": "SarahB",
            "timestamp": "15:48, 13 May 2015",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(16,940 bytes)",
            "change": -6,
            "summary": "(RCP reverted edits by 74.62.72.98 (talk) to  revision #17148961 by Votebot)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "When a fire first ignites, it may be small enough that you can extinguish it with a fire blanket or a fire extinguisher on hand. By being prepared and quickly determining the type of fire with which you’re dealing, you have an even better chance of not only extinguishing the fire but also of doing it without risking injury. However, remember that the safety of everyone in the proximity—including you—comes first. If the fire is quickly spreading, producing dangerous amounts of smoke, or taking longer than five seconds to defeat with a fire extinguisher, then you must [[Pull a Fire Alarm|pull a fire alarm]], evacuate the building, and call 911."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "When a fire first ignites, it may be hismall enough that you can extinguish it with a fire blanket or a fire extinguisher on hand. By being prepared and quickly determining the type of fire with which you’re dealing, you have an even better chance of not only extinguishing the fire but also of doing it without risking injury. However, remember that the safety of everyone in the proximity—including you—hicomes first. If the fire is quickly spreading, producing dangerous amounts of smoke, or taking longer than five seconds to defeat with a fire extinguisher, then you must [[Pull a Fire Alarm|hipull a fire alarm]], evacuate the building, and call 911."
                ]
            }
        },
