        {
            "id": "11048922",
            "user": "Souphead",
            "timestamp": "18:13, 8 September 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,705 bytes)",
            "change": -117,
            "summary": "(Formatting)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "=== General Rules ===",
                    "#[[Image:Forestfire.jpg|thumb|Fires are incredibly dangerous. Remember, don't fight a fire not worth fighting.]]'''If a fire has taken hold,''' (spread to other areas or structures or the smoke has is at head height), all occupants of that building must be [[Pull a Fire Alarm|alerted]] and evacuated. Note to all: Do not fight a fire not worth fighting, evacuate the building; personal safety comes first.",
                    "#Contact the fire brigade as soon as possible. [[Image:Firedep_536.jpg|thumb|Always contact the fire brigade if there is a incident of fire.]]",
                    "=== Electrical Fires ===",
                    "",
                    "The first thing you must do to stop",
                    "or smother a electrical fault or short circuit fire is to:",
                    "#The safest and the best way to extinguish a electrical fire is to use a fire extinguisher. Follow the instructions on it for more information. '''Note''': During the initial stages of fire, once the fire is put out the surfaces that it has touched or burned are very hot and are likely to catch fire. You must have enough extinguishing material for at least 7 extinguishing attempts. E.G. 6Kg dry powder fire extinguisher. (Remember: If you do not turn of the mains power supply, electric shock is likely to occur).Aim the extinguisher nozzle at the base or seat of a fire for most effective extinguishing of fire.",
                    "=== Liquid/Oil fires ===",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "The first thing you must do to stop a liquid fire (e.g gasoline, petrol) is to:",
                    "#Water does not mix with the oil, and thus goes underneath it (oil and water don't mix; when they are together oil is on top water on bottom). It will then boil and become steam '''very quickly''' this quick boil is dangerous . Since the water is on the bottom of the oil, as it evaporates it sprays hot, burning oil in every direction. This then spreads the fire and usually leaves you unconscious.",
                    "#Use a fire extinguisher. Remember to read the instructions very carefully. If the extinguisher instructs you to not use it on a liquid or chemical fire, do not use it. Call the fire brigade and evacuate the building. [[Image:Fireorg_94.jpg|thumb|Organic fires are the most common fires around. They must be dealt with quickly to stop spread.]]",
                    "=== Organic Fires ===",
                    "",
                    "The easiest fires to stop an organic fire in its initial stages (e.g. wood, paper) is to:",
                    "#Use a safety blanket to smother the fire in its early stages. Remember to cover the fire completely and wait ten minutes.",
                    "#Use a fire extinguisher; most will work on organic fires. Never forget to read the instructions on the extinguisher. Follow them to the letter.",
                    "=== Gas Fires ===",
                    "",
                    "[[Image:Firesmoke_784.jpg|thumb|Gas fires are the most dangerous fires. With high chances of explosion, you must always call the fire brigade in any gas fire or threat.]]'''Gas fires must always be carried out by the fire brigade.'''",
                    "#  If you smell gas or see a dangerous situation involving flammable gas, or any gas for that matter contact the fire brigade immediately, and evacuate to a safe distance.",
                    "*Fires are dangerous phenomenon. Do not fight a fire that is out of hand, and not worth fighting for. '''Your life comes first.''' Evacuate if the fire has spread and there is a low chance of extinguishing it by normal means and do not take the time to gather any belongings. Speed is essential."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Forestfire.jpg|thumb|Fires are incredibly dangerous. Remember, don't fight a fire not worth fighting.]]'''General rule: If a fire has taken hold,''' (spread to other areas or structures or the smoke has is at head height), all occupants of that building must be [[Pull a Fire Alarm|alerted]] and evacuated. Note to all: Do not fight a fire not worth fighting, evacuate the building, personal safety comes first.",
                    "# [[Image:Firedep_536.jpg|thumb|Always contact the fire brigade if there is a incident of fire..]]'''Electrical Fires:''' The first thing you must do to stop or smother a electrical fault or short circuit fire is to:",
                    "#The safest and the best way to extinguish a electrical fire is to use a fire extinguisher. Follow the instructions on it for more information. '''Note''': During the initial stages of fire, once the fire is put out the surfaces that it has touched or burned are very hot and are likely to catch fire. You must have enough extinguishing material for at least 7 extinguishing attempts. E.G. 6Kg dry powder fire extinguisher. (Remember: If you do not turn of the mains power supply, electric shock is likely to occur.)",
                    "#General rule: Aim the extinguisher nozzle at the base or seat of a fire for most effective extinguishing of fire.",
                    "# [[Image:Fireoil_242.jpg|thumb|Oil fires are very dangerous. It is recommended that you call the fire brigade at any time where there is an oil fire threat.]]'''Liquid fires:''' the first thing you must do to stop a liquid fire (e.g gasoline, petrol) is to:",
                    "#Water does not mix with the oil, and thus goes underneath it (oil and water don't mix, when they are together oil is on top water on bottom). It will then boil and become steam '''very quickly''' this quick boil is dangerous . Since the water is on the bottom of the oil, as it evaporates it sprays hot, burning oil in every direction. This then spreads the fire and usually leaves you unconscious.",
                    "#To stop a liquid fire you must:",
                    "#Use a fire extinguisher. Remember to read the instructions very carefully. If the extinguisher instructs you to not use it on a liquid or chemical fire, do not use it. Call the fire brigade and evacuate the building.",
                    "# [[Image:Fireorg_94.jpg|thumb|Organic fires are the most common fires around. They must be dealt with quickly to stop spread.]]'''Organic fires:''' The easiest fires to stop an organic fire in its initial stages (e.g. wood, paper) is to:",
                    "#Use a safety blanket to smother the fire in its early stages. Remember cover the fire completely and wait ten minutes.",
                    "#Use a fire extinguisher, most will work on organic fires. Never forget to read the instructions on the extinguisher. Follow them to the letter.",
                    "#*[[Image:Firesmoke_784.jpg|thumb|Gas fires are the most dangerous fires. With high chances of explosion, you must always call the fire brigade in any gas fire or threat.]]'''Gas fires must always be carried out by the fire brigade.''' If you smell gas or see a dangerous situation involving flammable gas, or any gas for that matter contact the fire brigade immediately, and evacuate to a safe distance.",
                    "*Fires are dangerous phenomenon do not fight a fire that is out of hand, and not worth fighting for. '''Your life comes first.''' Evacuate if the fire has spread and there is a low chance of extinguishing it by normal means and do not take the time to gather any belongings. Speed is essential."
                ]
            }
        },
